{"author":{"id":"dec876858d28babbadcead4137861bf586626b8518cbce9c5f4fc52ac3b424b8"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1791195262,"nonce":"JzU5BlK0XrVG3doMklb364gFg6Q=","title":"Repeated emoji are dropped: a😀😀b draws one smiley and leaves a blank","message":"Observed with TextRenderer::prepare_text_with_style, counting glyphs drawn:\n\n    a😀b         3 drawn, 3 visible characters\n    a😀😀b       3 drawn, 4 visible    (one smiley missing)\n    a😀😀😀b     3 drawn, 5 visible    (two missing)\n    a❤❤b         3 drawn, 4 visible    (one missing)\n    a☀️b         3 drawn, 3 visible    (correct: sun + variation selector)\n\nThe renderer skipped any emoji whose codepoint equalled the previous glyph's,\nto catch HarfBuzz reporting two glyphs for one cluster (a symbol and its\nvariation selector both carry the cluster's first codepoint). That test is on\nthe codepoint alone, so it also swallowed real repeats, while layout still\nreserved their space: a blank gap where a glyph should be.\n\nThe cluster is what tells them apart. A shaper duplicate shares the previous\nglyph's cluster; a repeat the author typed starts a new one.\n\nFixed together with 124ad5d, because layout and the renderer have to share one\nrule: layout must leave a skipped glyph's advance alone, and the renderer must\nskip exactly the same glyphs. Both now call one function,\nfallback::is_cluster_duplicate.\n\nFound while moving the renderer's fallback logic into layout, after a probe of\nhow many glyphs came out for repeated emoji. It had been there as long as the\nrule.","files":null}]}